Chapter: LapicRegs

  cites Kernel chapter Lapic

 It demands a specific number. The version register reports 0x00050014
 about itself, and that constant is the whole point of testing it: an
 unmapped device gigabyte, an unserved MMIO exit and a stub body all
 answer 0, and so does a legitimately-empty ISR. A test that only
 asserted the drain returns 0 would pass on a driver that does nothing
 at all -- the block-identify trap, where a function that always answers
 the same thing looks exactly like one that works. The version is the one
 register here that a broken path cannot fake.

 The SIVR round trip is the write half, and it is the sharper of the two:
 a value the guest chose comes back only if a real write reached the
 decoder and a real read found it there. It read back 0xFE for a while,
 because the emulator's MMIO write path took the value from RAX rather
 than from the register the store named, and no guest driver had ever
 written a LAPIC register whose value mattered.

Section: Definitions

  test-version : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  test-version = act
    v <- lapic-version
    if v == #50014 then 1 else 0
  end

  test-sivr-roundtrip : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  test-sivr-roundtrip = act
    w <- lapic-set-sivr #1FF
    v <- lapic-sivr
    if v == #1FF then 1 else 0
  end

  test-bsp-id : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  test-bsp-id = act
    v <- lapic-id
    if v == 0 then 1 else 0
  end

  test-drain-quiet : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  test-drain-quiet = act
    n <- lapic-drain
    if n == 0 then 1 else 0
  end

  opening : [Console, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    a <- test-version
    b <- test-sivr-roundtrip
    c <- test-bsp-id
    d <- test-drain-quiet
    print-line-uni (integer-to-text (a + b + c + d))
  end
